Q: crypto-js DES (or Triple DES) encryption in CBC Mode A: <p>Found it !</p>

<pre>
&lt;script type="text/javascript" src="http://crypto-js.googlecode.com/files/2.5.3-crypto-sha1-hmac-pbkdf2-blockmodes-DES.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script type="text/javascript"&gt;

// CBC with default padding scheme (iso7816)
var crypted = Crypto.DES.encrypt("Message", "Secret Passphrase", { mode: new Crypto.mode.CBC });
var plain = Crypto.DES.decrypt(crypted, "Secret Passphrase", { mode: new Crypto.mode.CBC });

// CBC with ansix923 padding scheme
var crypted = Crypto.DES.encrypt("Message", "Secret Passphrase", { mode: new Crypto.mode.CBC(Crypto.pad.ansix923) });
var plain = Crypto.DES.decrypt(crypted, "Secret Passphrase", { mode: new Crypto.mode.CBC(Crypto.pad.ansix923) });

&lt;/script&gt;
</pre>

Q: AVR gcc creates intermediate counting variable when not needed A: <p>I have just compiled your code with avr-gcc v14.3 (after addinf some missing headers):</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stdint.h&gt; // Added because otherwise the code doesn't compile.
#include &lt;avr/interrupt.h&gt; // dito

void function(uint8_t byte){
    cli();

    uint8_t bit = 0x01;
    while (1) {
        //    for (uint8_t bit = 0x01; bit != 0; bit &lt;&lt;=1) {

        if (byte &amp; bit) { // Write high
            // do something not using byte or bit variables
        } else { // Write low
            // do something else not using byte or bit variables
        }
        bit = bit &lt;&lt; 1;
        if (bit == 0)
            break;
    }

    sei();
}
</code></pre>
<p>The generated assembly is:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>function:
/* #APP */
    cli 
    sei 
/* #NOAPP */
/* epilogue start */
    ret
</code></pre>
<p>So everything is as expected.</p>
<p>Maybe you have a bug in your Makefile, and you are seeing code generated from an outdated source file.</p>
<p>Apaft from that, except for the <code>cli</code> and <code>sei</code>, your code doesn't have any side effects.  Maybe you had some other code in your past buffer than on file.</p>
<hr />
<h2>A working Test Case</h2>
<p>So here is a test case that might be what you have seen somewhere:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;

char x;

void func (uint8_t byte)
{
    for (uint8_t bit = 0x01; bit != 0; bit &lt;&lt;= 1)
        if (byte &amp; bit)
            x = 0;
        else
            x = 1;
}
</code></pre>
<p>It omits <code>cli</code> and <code>sei</code> because they have no effect on the optimization. With <code>-Os -mmcu=avr32dd14</code> avr-gcc v14 generates this assembly:</p>
<pre class="lang-lisp prettyprint-override"><code>    ldi r18,lo8(8)   ;  ivtmp_4,
    ldi r19,0        ;  ivtmp_4
 ;  x.c:29:     for (uint8_t bit = 0x01; bit != 0; bit &lt;&lt;= 1)
    ldi r25,lo8(1)   ;  bit,
.L3:
 ;  x.c:30:         if (byte &amp; bit)
    ldi r21,lo8(1)   ;  _3,
    mov r20,r24  ;  _1, byte
    and r20,r25  ;  _1, bit
    cpse r20,__zero_reg__    ;  _1
    ldi r21,0        ;  _3
.L2:
 ;  x.c:29:     for (uint8_t bit = 0x01; bit != 0; bit &lt;&lt;= 1)
    lsl r25      ;  bit
 ;  x.c:29:     for (uint8_t bit = 0x01; bit != 0; bit &lt;&lt;= 1)
    subi r18, 1  ;  ivtmp_4,
    sbci r19, 0  ;  ivtmp_4,
    brne .L3         ; ,
    sts x,r21    ;  x, _3
</code></pre>
<p>As you can see, the compiler introduces a 16-bit induction variable <code>ivtmp_4</code> that runs from 8 to 0.  In many cases, introducing such induction variables leads to less efficient code than a naive loop, cf. <a href="https://gcc.gnu.org/PR116548" rel="nofollow noreferrer">PR116548</a>.  For example, with <code>-fno-tree-loop-optimize</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-lisp prettyprint-override"><code> ;  x.c:29:     for (uint8_t bit = 0x01; bit != 0; bit &lt;&lt;= 1)
    ldi r25,lo8(1)   ;  bit,
.L3:
 ;  x.c:30:         if (byte &amp; bit)
    ldi r19,lo8(1)   ;  _15,
    mov r18,r24  ;  _1, byte
    and r18,r25  ;  _1, bit
    cpse r18,__zero_reg__    ;  _1
    ldi r19,0        ;  _15
.L2:
 ;  x.c:29:     for (uint8_t bit = 0x01; bit != 0; bit &lt;&lt;= 1)
    lsl r25      ;  bit
 ;  x.c:29:     for (uint8_t bit = 0x01; bit != 0; bit &lt;&lt;= 1)
    cpse r25,__zero_reg__    ;  bit,
    rjmp .L3     ; 
    sts x,r19    ;  x, _15
</code></pre>
<p>(The 2nd <code>cpse</code> is fallout from the cc0→CCmode transition; avr-gcc 8 uses <code>lsl r25</code> + <code>brne .L3</code>)</p>
<p>As an aside, this test case doesn't work with avr-gcc v16 which just generates</p>
<pre class="lang-lisp prettyprint-override"><code> ;  x.c:30:         if (byte &amp; bit)
    cpi r24,0x80     ;  byte
    sbc r24,r24  ;  _10
    neg r24  ;  _10
    sts x,r24    ;  x, _10
</code></pre>
